digraph ReasoningTree {
  rankdir="TB";
  "N10" [label="(12,0,0) If molecular-scale photostructural changes generate untenable crystal strain, then macroscopic deformations (peel, bend, stretch, crack, fracture, explode) can occur under light."];
  "N12" [label="(14,0,0) For photonic encoding, a [RuSO2] complex ideally achieves 100% SO2-linkage photoisomerisation so dark (0) and light-induced (1,2,...) states are cleanly distinguishable as integer encodings."];
  "N14" [label="(16,0,0) The η2-(OS)O photoisomer is often found in [RuSO2], but it has never been structurally isolated at 100% photoconversion; instead it co-exists with η1-OSO and/or η1-SO2."];
  "N15" [label="(0,0,0) If the goal is high-purity multi-state (e.g., ternary) photonic signatures in a single-crystal actuator, then one should seek a system that can (i) reach 100% photoconversion into distinct photoisomeric crystal structures, and (ii) couple that photostructural change to macroscopic actuation via strain."];
  "N20" [label="(18,0,0) Compound 1 undergoes 100% SO2-linkage photoisomerisation from η1-SO2 dark state to a pure η1-OSO crystal at 90 K or to a pure η2-(OS)O crystal at 100 K."];
  "N21" [label="(19,0,0) Each 100%-converted photoisomer in 1 is metastable at its temperature and is accompanied by substantial micro/nanoscopic strain that diminishes upon thermal recovery to the η1-SO2 dark state."];
  "N5" [label="(5,0,0) Single-crystal optical actuation has been demonstrated across many compound classes (organic, inorganic, organometallic), so the phenomenon is broadly feasible."];
  "N6" [label="(6,0,0) Coordination complexes with transition metals (e.g., Ru) are especially interesting because MLCT gives broad-band optical absorption while maintaining good thermal stability."];
  "N8" [label="(10,0,0) Many [RuSO2] variants can support η1-OSO and/or η2-(OS)O photoisomers in single crystals; the light-induced structures are metastable at low temperature (~100 K) and thermally revert to the η1-SO2 dark state."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) Develop and evaluate the new single-crystal [RuSO2] complex trans-[Ru(SO2)(NH3)4(4-bromopyridine)](tosylate)2 as a reversible, temperature-controlled ternary optical actuator by testing whether visible-light-induced 100% SO2-linkage photoisomerisation into η1-OSO and η2-(OS)O states, together with thermal recovery and crystal strain, can generate clean multi-state photonic signatures.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) A material platform that addresses prior [RuSO2] limitations, reaches clean fully converted photoisomeric states, and couples photostructural changes to crystal strain motivates development and evaluation as a multi-state single-crystal optical actuator."];
  "R1" [label="(0,0,0) For [RuSO2] photonic encoding, achieving clean 100% SO2-linkage photoisomerisation into distinct light-induced states is a key route toward high-purity multi-state photonic signatures."];
  "R11" [label="(0,0,0) Compound 1's access to three fully converted SO2-linkage structures supports testing whether it can deliver high-purity ternary photonic signatures in a single-crystal optical actuator."];
  "R4" [label="(0,0,0) Compound 1's low-temperature access to pure η1-SO2, η1-OSO, and η2-(OS)O crystal structures supports interpreting its switching as SO2-linkage photostructural ternary switching."];
  "R6" [label="(0,0,0) In compound 1, substantial strain accompanying fully converted photoisomers could drive light-induced macroscopic deformation such as cracking, making cracking a relevant optical-actuation response to evaluate."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) Ru-containing coordination complexes are plausible candidates for developing single-crystal optical actuators because single-crystal optical actuation is broadly feasible and Ru complexes offer useful optical absorption and thermal stability."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) For compound 1, thermal recovery can provide a route back toward the η1-SO2 dark-state structure after formation of metastable light-induced photoisomers."];
  "N14" -> "N15" [label="induction-case"];
  "N12" -> "N15" [label="induction-common"];
  "N14" -> "R1" [label="induction-case"];
  "N12" -> "R1" [label="induction-common"];
